  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the roots of $g(z)=\\frac{{z}^{3}+5{z}^{2}-z-5}{{z}^{3}+6{z}^{2}+5z}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The roots of $g(z)$ are the solutions to the equation $g(z)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{z}^{3}+5{z}^{2}-z-5}{{z}^{3}+6{z}^{2}+5z}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(z) & =\\frac{{z}^{3}+5{z}^{2}-z-5}{{z}^{3}+6{z}^{2}+5z} \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{2}(z+5)-(z+5)}{z({z}^{2}+6z+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({z}^{2}-1)(z+5)}{z(z+1)(z+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(z+1)(z-1)(z+5)}{z(z+1)(z+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(z-1)\\cancel{(z+1)(z+5)}}{z\\cancel{(z+1)(z+5)}} &  \\\\  & =\\frac{z-1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1=0\\,⟹\\,z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $g(z)=0$ is $z=1.$",
